# Target C — two-half-disk remainder/sign localization

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Status:** proof-only Type-II composition; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.

This note combines the proof-only all-regime two-half-disk alternative with the
audited component remainder of Sections 160--161 and the exact sign identity of
Section 172. It gives a source-level lower bound for the harmonic diameter
moment plus retained remainder, and therefore a uniform lower bound on how
negative the long-hull-side correction can be in that branch.

It is deliberately a sign localization, not a closure: the resulting lower
bound still permits a linear outer-walk surplus or a positive hull-side
correction.

## 1. Inputs and notation

Work on a finite diagonal in the proof-only two-half-disk alternative. Let
\(\mathcal G_1,\mathcal G_2\) be the two disjoint dominant harmonic-cell
groups. The groupwise train theorem gives

\[
\sum_{r\in\mathcal G_i} A_{4,r}
=\left(\frac{\pi}{8}-o(1)\right)D^2,
\qquad i=1,2,
\tag{173.1}
\]

where \(A_{4,r}\) is the strict-rhombus area assigned to harmonic cell \(r\).

Use the Section-160 quantities

\[
S_D:=\sum_r d_r,
\qquad
G_D:=R_I+s_B+\sum_r\eta_r
=2+q+m-k-S_D,
\tag{173.2}
\]

and the Section-172 hull-side correction

\[
\chi_D:=q+m-\operatorname{per}(F).
\tag{173.3}
\]

All sums below are over the unloaded positive-contact components underlying the
harmonic cells. Contact-isolated source points are not included in these core
quantities.

## 2. Global loaded-rhombus error is only linear

Let \(A_{4,r}^{\mathrm{int}}\) denote the strict-rhombus area in cell \(r\)
whose four vertices are unloaded and hence lie in one unloaded component.
Then

\[
\sum_{r\in\mathcal G_i} A_{4,r}^{\mathrm{int}}
=\left(\frac{\pi}{8}-o(1)\right)D^2,
\qquad i=1,2.
\tag{173.4}
\]

Here is the finite error check. A triangle-free unit-contact vertex has at
most five unit neighbors: the angles between consecutive unit-neighbor rays
are strictly greater than \(\pi/3\). Therefore the total number of local
strict rhombi incident to a loaded source vertex is \(O(1)\). The loaded
population on the force hull is \(q=O(D)\), and the audited local-rhombus
incidence check makes one physical local rhombus chargeable only a bounded
number of times. Each unit rhombus has area at most one. Consequently

\[
\sum_r\left(A_{4,r}-A_{4,r}^{\mathrm{int}}\right)=O(D).
\tag{173.5}
\]

This is the only loaded-interface estimate used here. It does not assert that
all local faces are global faces, and it does not delete contact-isolated
source points.

## 3. Summing the retained component inequalities

The audited finite Section-160 inequality is, for each unloaded component,

\[
r_r+\eta_r
\ge \sqrt{\pi A_{4,r}^{\mathrm{int}}}+2-d_r.
\tag{173.6}
\]

Rearrange and sum (173.6) over all components belonging to one dominant group.
The nonnegative terms \(r_r+\eta_r\) are subterms of \(G_D\), while the
corresponding \(d_r\) are subterms of \(S_D\). Since

\[
\sum_j\sqrt{a_j}\ge\sqrt{\sum_j a_j}
\qquad(a_j\ge0),
\tag{173.7}
\]

and each dominant group contains at least one positive-area component, (173.4)
and (173.6) imply

\[
\sum_{r\in\mathcal G_i}d_r
\;+\sum_{r\in\mathcal G_i}(r_r+\eta_r)
\ge
\sqrt{\pi\sum_{r\in\mathcal G_i}A_{4,r}^{\mathrm{int}}}
=\frac{\pi}{2\sqrt2}D-o(D).
\tag{173.8}
\]

The omitted \(+2\) terms are nonnegative and are not treated as a linear gain.

The two groups are disjoint. Adding (173.8), and then adding the remaining
nonnegative \(d_r\), \(r_r\), \(\eta_r\), and \(s_B\) terms, gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}\frac{S_D+G_D}{D}
\ge \frac{\pi}{\sqrt2}.
}
\tag{173.9}
\]

This is a same-subsequence estimate. It does not optimize a carrier area and a
component remainder taken from different configurations.

## 4. Consequence for the long-hull-side correction

From the exact definition of \(G_D\),

\[
\chi_D=S_D+G_D+k-2-\operatorname{per}(F).
\tag{173.10}
\]

In Type II, \(\operatorname{per}(F)/D\to\pi\). Therefore every two-half-disk
subsequence satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\liminf\frac{\chi_D}{D}
\ge \frac{\pi}{\sqrt2}-\pi+\liminf\frac{k}{D}
=-\pi\left(1-\frac1{\sqrt2}\right)
\ +\liminf\frac{k}{D}.
}
\tag{173.11}
\]

Numerically the base bound is

\[
\frac{\pi}{\sqrt2}-\pi=-0.920151184510610\ldots.
\tag{173.12}
\]

Thus the two-half-disk branch cannot realize an arbitrarily negative
long-hull-side correction. Combining with the exact outer-walk identity

\[
\frac{b-\operatorname{per}(F)}{D}
=\frac{u_B+\chi_D}{D}\ge0
\tag{173.13}
\]

gives the valid dichotomy:

- if \(\chi_D\ge0\), the five-currency correction is favorable; or
- if \(\chi_D<0\), the outer-walk surplus satisfies
  \(u_B/D\ge-\chi_D/D\), while (173.11) bounds the possible deficit by
  \(0.920152-\liminf(k/D)\).

No conclusion that \(u_B=o(D)\) or \(\chi_D\ge0\) is made.

## 5. Frontier implication

The two-half-disk alternative now has the source-level lower bound

\[
\frac{S_D+G_D}{D}\ge\frac{\pi}{\sqrt2}-o(1),
\]

in addition to its groupwise atomic train floor. A complete closure would still
need an upper bound on the same aggregate outer-walk detour, a theorem turning
the forced boundary surplus into a strict source-preserving diameter decrease,
or an exact unit equilateral triangle. The center-wrapping branch and the
macroscopic Type-I branch are untouched.

**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting promotion; audited-working
proof-only Type-II branch theorem. C-II remains open.
